            "Convert an assembly program with three-address code into a register-register two-address program.",
          body: `Input three-address code: t1 = A + B; t2 = C + D; t3 = t1 − t2. The two-address register format only has one source and one destination operand, so each statement must end in a register that holds the final value. First, copy the first sum into a register: R1 = A + B (t1 is now held by R1). Then add the second sum: R1 = R1 + C + D is not valid as written, so restructure: R2 = C + D, then R1 = R1 − R2. The final result is held in R1. This rewrite costs one extra register and requires a temporary, which is the classic reason compilers dislike two-address form for complex expressions. A one-pass register allocation scheme must plan for this temporary. The worked transformation shows why the compiler must allocate registers carefully and why the intermediate results need distinct registers when the expression tree is deep.`,
