          body: `A load instruction uses base register R10 = 1000, index register R11 = 4, and displacement = 20, with addressing mode base + index × scale 2. Effective address = base + index × scale + displacement = 1000 + 8 + 20 = 1028. If the scale were 1 instead of 2, the address would be 1024; if the index were absent, it would be 1020. The instruction then reads memory at the effective address and writes the value to the destination register. The arithmetic on the effective address is exactly what the address generation unit computes combinationally, and the address is used to index the memory or to read a register. This worked example is the kind of arithmetic GATE expects from students, and it demonstrates that you should always expand the mode before adding. The trap is to compute (base + index) × scale instead of base + (index × scale), which changes the address by a factor of the scale for every index value.`,
