            "Estimate the cycles for a single instruction in a five-stage pipeline versus a single-cycle processor.",
          body: `A simple arithmetic instruction in a five-stage pipeline takes one cycle per stage in the ideal case, so four cycles for a register-register ALU instruction if write-back is merged into execute, or five if the pipeline register is explicit; a load takes the full five stages; a branch takes fetch, decode, execute to condition, and then target fetch, which is three stages but with control dependency. In a single-cycle processor, every instruction takes the same number of cycles, set by the slowest instruction, typically a load or a branch. For a program with 100 arithmetic instructions and 10 loads, single-cycle: (100 × 2 + 10 × 5) = 250 cycles (if arithmetic takes 2 and loads take 5). Pipelined with ideal handling: arithmetic 100 × 2 = 200 cycles of actual work plus 10 load stalls of 2 cycles (load-use) = 220 cycles, achieving near-ideal throughput of 210 cycles for the full program including fill time. The speedup is roughly 250/210 ≈ 1.19 for this small program, but the pipeline's value grows with program length. The worked example shows how to count and how to include stalls in the pipelined case.`,
