          answer: `The instruction is t = A + B, where t is the destination and A, B are the sources; that is three operand fields plus the opcode. The correct choice is (C) Three. The three-address format carries two source registers and one destination register, so three operand fields are required. The single-operand register/immediate format would carry two fields (destination and source/immediate), a memory-register format would carry one register and one address, and the accumulator format would carry one source. Choice (B) would be a two-address format where one field is both source and destination. The format determines how many register fields are needed, which in turn drives instruction width and encoding cost.`,
