        body: `Every choice in this chapter moves the three performance variables of the CPU performance equation. Single-cycle designs make clock slow because they wait for the slowest instruction; multi-cycle designs allow a faster clock by completing instructions in phases, at the cost of a longer control state machine and longer per-instruction time. Hardwired control is faster per phase than microprogrammed control because it avoids control memory access, but adding instructions is harder. Deeper pipelines reduce clock time but increase the penalty of a mispredicted branch and of a data hazard that must be resolved by forwarding. Out-of-order execution increases complexity and uses more registers and resources to hide latency, but reduces the effective CPI for latency-bound code. The performance engineer evaluates these trade-offs by the resulting instruction count, CPI, and clock frequency. The GATE-level skill is to be able to trace a design decision through all three variables and to identify which design produces which performance outcome.`,
