        body: `Design A: hardwired, two-phase (execute and write-back combined). Clock period: 4 ns. Design B: microprogrammed, two-phase. Control requires fetching a microinstruction from control memory, then decoding it and asserting signals, taking three clock cycles per phase; the clock period is 3 ns because each phase is still one cycle. For a program with 100 instructions, Design A: 200 ns. Design B: 200 instructions × 2 phases × 3 clocks = 1200 ns, a 6× slowdown from control overhead alone, before accounting for any other differences. If the program were memory-bound, the control overhead might be swamped, but for small programs it dominates. The worked example shows why control implementation choice matters at the instruction-count level, and it is why microprogramming is often reserved for complex instructions or where flexibility matters more than raw speed. A hardwired design with 4 ns clock versus a microprogrammed design with 3 ns clock: the hardwired design completes each instruction in two phases per clock, so the effective CPI is 2 for both, but the hardwired design uses one clock per phase and the microprogrammed design takes one clock per phase as well, making the difference the control fetch overhead, which hardwired avoids.`,
