        body: `Load instruction: lw R1, 0(R2) — R1 = contents of memory at address in R2. Phase sequence: fetch (PC increments), decode (register reads asserted, address from R2), execute (memory read enable asserted with address R2, ALU writes address not needed), memory (memory read completes), write-back (R1 written from memory data). The control signals during execute: memory read enable asserted, ALU write enable false, register read enable asserted; during write-back: memory read enable false, register write enable asserted with destination R1. The sequence is a four-phase sequence. In a three-phase design, the memory read and write-back are combined and the load takes three phases; the write enable happens at the end of the third phase. The key point is that the memory address and data flow through the control signals, and the write to the register file happens only in write-back, after the memory data has been captured. A control signal bug here would be writing the register before the memory data is ready, which is why phase ordering matters.`,
